course: medicine
lesson: 37
title: Warming up, stitches and cramp
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Cold muscles are like cold plasticine: stiff and quick to tear. A warm-up of jogging, skips and leg swings slowly raises your heart rate and warms the muscles so they stretch, react and grip better.
explain: A stitch is a sharp pain under the ribs while running. Nobody knows its exact cause, but a pie and a big fizzy drink just before the second half make it far more likely, and it fades once you slow down.
explain: Cramp is a muscle that suddenly tightens and won't let go, often a calf in the last tired minutes of a match. A slow, steady stretch, like pulling the toes up towards you, helps it relax.
explain: Neither a stitch nor cramp is damage, but a tummy pain that stays after you stop, or cramp in deep water, needs the right moves: tell an adult, or float on your back and call for help.
fact: A warm-up of about 10 minutes slowly raises the heart rate and warms the muscles; cold muscles strain more easily.
fact: Before sport a moving warm-up (jogging, skips, high knees, leg swings) works best; long held stretches are better kept for afterwards.
fact: Holding long stretches just before a sprint can make you slightly slower.
fact: On cold days, warm up longer and keep layers on until the whistle; keeping warm helps muscles work.
fact: A stitch is a sharp pain below the ribs during running; its exact cause is not known, it leaves no harm, and slowing down, breathing out slowly and pressing on the spot help.
fact: A full stomach or lots of sugary drink just before running makes a stitch more likely, so at half-time have a small snack and sips of water.
fact: A stitch fades within minutes when you stop; tummy pain that stays, gets worse, or comes with being sick or a fever is not a stitch and must be told to an adult.
fact: Cramp is a muscle suddenly tightening and not relaxing; tired, overworked muscles cramp more easily, and it is usually not a tear; it usually passes in minutes, though the muscle can ache afterwards.
fact: The causes of exercise cramp aren't fully known; lack of potassium has not been proven, so bananas are a fine snack but not a cramp cure.
fact: Night cramp in the calf is common and usually harmless; pulling the toes up towards you stretches it; if it happens very often, see a GP.
fact: The old rule of waiting an hour after eating before swimming, or you'll get cramp, has no good evidence behind it.
fact: A cramp must be stretched slowly and gently; jerking it hard can pull the muscle.
fact: If you get cramp in the water, roll onto your back, float, shout and wave for help, because panicking and thrashing tires you fast and you can swallow water.
exercise: Harry from Newcastle: "Howay, warm-ups are for softies." First sprint, he grabs his thigh. Why? | Cold muscles strain more easily | Warm-ups make muscles weaker and slower | He ran too slowly at first to get warm | Thighs can only hurt in winter
exercise: About how long is a sensible warm-up before a match? | About 10 minutes | About 10 seconds of jumping | About 2 hours, like the pros do | No time at all, just start playing
exercise: Chloe holds a toe-touch for a whole minute before the 60 m. What works better? | Jogging, skips and leg swings | A longer held stretch of five minutes | Sitting still on the bench to save energy | A big drink of fizzy pop
exercise: When are long held stretches best done? | After sport, to cool down | Right before a sprint, to loosen up | Instead of any warm-up, to save time | Only when you have cramp in a muscle
exercise: Callum in Glasgow at kick-off in the sleet, frozen. His mate calls him nesh (soft about cold). Best plan? | Warm up longer and keep layers on until kick-off | Strip off to toughen up, like the old players did | Skip the warm-up to keep his energy for the game | Stand still so heat stays in
exercise: Amara, 20 minutes in: "Like a knife in my side!" What is it most likely? | A stitch | A broken rib | Her appendix bursting inside her | A pulled muscle in her thigh
exercise: What helps a stitch go? | Slowing down, breathing out slowly, pressing the spot | Sprinting even faster to shake it off | Gulping down a big cold fizzy drink | Lying flat on the grass and holding your breath for a minute
exercise: Josh in Brisbane gets a meat pie and a big fizzy drink at the servo (petrol station) at half-time. What happens? | A stitch becomes more likely | He'll run faster in the second half | It stops both stitches and cramp for the rest of the day | Nothing, food can't affect running
exercise: Mia's side pain goes as soon as she stops. When would a tummy pain need an adult? | When it stays, gets worse, or comes with sickness or fever | Whenever it's on the right side, even if it goes | Only if she is crying | Never, every pain in sport is a stitch
exercise: Declan in Cork: "Me calf's locked solid!" in the last 10 minutes. What is cramp? | A muscle suddenly tightening and not relaxing | A torn muscle that needs stitches at the hospital | A broken bone in the leg | A blood vessel bursting in the muscle
exercise: Why does cramp often come near the end of a match? | Tired muscles cramp more easily | Muscles cool down and stiffen as the game goes on | The grass gets wet and slippery by then | Players stop breathing in the last minutes
exercise: Ethan's mum: "Three bananas and you'll never cramp." True? | Not proven; bananas are a snack, not a cure | Yes, the potassium in bananas always stops cramp | Yes, but only with five bananas | No, bananas cause cramp
exercise: Sophie wakes at 2 am with a rock-hard calf. What helps? | Pull her toes up towards her to stretch the calf | Point her toes down as hard as she can and hold | Rub ice on it for a whole hour | Run downstairs as fast as possible
exercise: Noah in Sydney, in his togs (swimsuit), gets calf cramp in the deep end. Safest move? | Roll onto his back, float, shout and wave | Swim flat out to the edge | Dive down under the water and rub the muscle hard | Hold his breath and keep still under water
exercise: Zara's friend yanks her cramped thigh up hard: "Gotta snap it out, eh!" Right? | No, a cramp needs a slow, gentle stretch | Yes, the harder you pull it, the better | Yes, a quick jerk is the fastest and safest way | No, cramp should never be stretched
exercise: Noah is 12 m along a 25 m pool. How far to the other end? | 13 m | 37 m | 12 m | 25 m
test: "Warm-ups are long, just stretch your hammies for a min innit." Why is a moving warm-up better? | It raises the heart rate and warms the muscles | Long holds make you faster before sprints | Moving tires you out before the game | Stretching cold muscles is the safest way
test: Bairns (children) at a Scottish match stand around in the dreich (cold, wet, grey) weather between halves. What should they do? | Keep moving and keep layers on | Take their tops off to cool down between the halves | Sit down on the wet grass to rest their legs | Nothing, cold doesn't affect muscles
test: "Bruv, a stitch means summat's ripped inside." True? | No, it fades and leaves no harm | Yes, a stitch is a torn gut that has to heal | Yes, it's the appendix every single time | No, it's caused by the shoes
test: What would happen if Josh had a small snack and sips of water instead of the pie? | A stitch would be less likely | He would surely get cramp from hunger instead | He'd have no energy at all for the second half | He'd be sick for certain
test: "Crap, my side's killing me and I stopped ten minutes ago and I feel sick." What's right? | Tell an adult, it may not be a stitch | Keep running, it's just a stitch that's hanging on | Eat a banana and ignore it till the end | Drink fizzy pop to settle it
test: In a game, cramp knocks a player out for the whole season. Real life? | Cramp usually passes in minutes, though it can ache after | Cramp always tears the muscle it hits | Cramp means a bone underneath is broken | Cramp can never happen to pros
test: Coach yells: "Shake it out, give that leg a proper yank!" What's sound advice? | Stretch slowly and steadily, never with a jerk | Yank it as hard as possible to snap the cramp out | Keep it bent and tight until it stops on its own | Sprint on it until it stops
test: "Swim within an hour of eating and you'll get cramp and drown, my nan said." Truth? | There's no good evidence for the one-hour rule | It's proven, you always have to wait a full hour | You must wait three hours after a big meal | Only fruit causes cramp in water
test: Dad, half asleep: "What the hell's happening?" Sophie's night cramp. Is it serious? | Usually harmless; see a GP if it happens very often | Always a sign of a serious illness in the blood | It means she must stop all sport for a year | It means she is short of bananas
test: Cramp hits mid-swim, halfway across. What would happen if he panicked and thrashed? | He'd tire fast and could swallow water | The cramp would vanish faster | He would sink straight to the bottom of the pool | Nothing, thrashing is the safe choice
test: Mate: "I eat bare bananas so I'm cramp-proof, no cap." (bare: lots of) Right? | No, cramp can still come, mostly from tired muscles | Yes, bananas make you cramp-proof | Yes, as long as he eats ten a day | No, bananas cause cramp
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a warm-up undertaken prior to physical exertion. | To raise the heart rate and warm the muscles in preparation | To fatigue the muscles in advance of competition | To lower the temperature of the body before exertion | To render any subsequent stretching of the muscles unnecessary
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the abdominal pain termed a stitch. | Its precise cause is unknown and it subsides upon slowing down | It invariably denotes the rupture of an internal organ | It is invariably caused by a deficiency of potassium in the diet | It persists for several days in all instances
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate response to a cramp arising in the calf muscle. | A slow and steady stretch, drawing the toes towards the body | A sudden and forceful jerk of the affected limb | The immediate consumption of three bananas | Complete abstention from movement for a week
exam: It is given that a swimmer experiences cramp in deep water. Determine the recommended course of action. | To float upon the back and summon assistance | To swim with maximum effort towards the edge of the pool | To submerge in order to massage the muscle | To remain motionless beneath the surface
